626 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 307 0 R
/E (When Control Design Serves Human Assistance and Rehabilitation with Exoskeletons)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
627 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 307 0 R
/E (ABSTRACT)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
628 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 307 0 R
/E (Assisting older adults and people with disabilities in their daily activities through wearable robotic technologies has attracted growing interest, driven by the increasing prevalence of mobility impairments and the rapid aging of the global population. Advances in compact components, low-power electronics, lightweight actuators, and accessible sensing are making assistive devices more wearable, autonomous and affordable, and exoskeletons, wearable biosensors and functional electrical stimulation systems are steadily moving from research prototypes to practical applications. In this context, control plays a central role in enabling wearable robots to interact safely, intuitively, and effectively with the human body. This talk reviews major challenges and recent advances in wearable robotics, with a focus on control strategies for assistive and rehabilitation exoskeletons, illustrated through numerical simulations and real-time experiments involving human subjects.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
629 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 307 0 R
/E (ABOUT THE SPEAKER)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
630 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 307 0 R
/E (Prof. Ahmed Chemori received his M.Sc. and PhD degrees in Automatic Control from the Polytechnic Institute of Grenoble, France, in 2001 and 2005. He is currently a Senior CNRS Researcher in Automatic Control and Robotics at the LIRMM laboratory, University of Montpellier. His research focuses on nonlinear control, including adaptive, robust and predictive control, with applications to wearable, parallel and marine robotics. He has authored or co-authored more than 200 scientific publications and has co-supervised 28 PhD theses and over 40 M.Sc. theses. He leads the Marine Robotics Team and chairs the LIRMM Valorization Committee. He serves as Technical Editor for IEEE/ASME Transactions on Mechatronics, is an IEEE Senior Member, and is a member of several IFAC Technical Committees.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
