677 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 310 0 R
/E (The Indian robotics industry is undergoing a structural transition from automation consumption to indigenous product innovation across manufacturing, healthcare, defence, agri-tech and logistics - from an automation user to a creator of Indian-origin robots. While the emerging market promises immense employability, a critical gap persists between conventional robotics education and the fast-evolving needs of industry. This session highlights the paradigm shift required in learning, from component-based theory to a systems approach centred on Physical-AI, supported by shared laboratory infrastructure, industry-academia co-creation and a curriculum built around real-world systems. The discussion will delineate the employability contours of the emerging market and propose a cohesive model to align research, pedagogy and Indian-origin robotics innovation.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
678 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 310 0 R
/E (ABOUT THE SPEAKER)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
679 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 310 0 R
/E (Prof. Ekta Singla is the Director of NAMTech School of Robotics, an education initiative from ArcelorMittal in Gujarat, and Secretary of The Robotics Society \(TRS\) India. A former Head of the Mechanical Engineering Department at IIT Ropar, she holds a PhD in Robotics from IIT Kanpur and has over two decades of academic and research experience, including postdoctoral work at TU Berlin and UPMC Paris and a visiting professorship at SUNY Stony Brook. Her research spans modular and reconfigurable robotics, healthcare and exoskeleton systems, and robotic assistance in vertical farming, and wearable exoskeletons. A member of the CII National Committee on Robotics and Automation, she contributes to national policy discussions on robotics education. Her accolades include a national award, a Director's gold medal and fellowships from DFG Germany and CNRS France. She has served on the Governing Council of The Robotics Society since 2021 and founded the PUNJRobotics platform at IIT Ropar in 2014.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
