756 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 315 0 R
/E (ABSTRACT)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
757 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 315 0 R
/E (Minimally invasive surgery \(MIS\) has transformed patient outcomes, but it places significant demands on the surgeon: restricted access, limited visual feedback, reliance on image guidance and interaction with soft, deforming tissue. Robotics can improve dexterity and precision and, increasingly, take on parts of the surgical workflow autonomously. This talk presents our work from robotic assistance to autonomous interventions: a CT-guided robotic system for accurate instrument placement in neurosurgery; AutoPlan, which performs fully autonomous trajectory planning for steerable needles; autonomous endoscope positioning by a cobot in laparoscopic surgery; and the open problem of manipulating deformable objects, using physics-based models with learned dynamics and planning.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
758 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 315 0 R
/E (ABOUT THE SPEAKER)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [10]
>>
