790 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 317 0 R
/E (Advances in biomechanics are transforming our ability to understand, predict and enhance human movement, health, and performance. This talk presents recent research at the intersection of multi-scale computational biomechanics, with predictive healthcare examples, and human-centered engineering through wearable robotic technologies. By combining physics-based modeling, scientific AI, and experimental human studies, biomechanical frameworks can predict injury risk, characterize disease progression, and optimize personalized interventions. The talk also highlights the development of wearable robotic systems, including soft exosuits and assistive technologies, with emphasis on human-in-the-loop modeling, neuromuscular dynamics, and digital-twin approaches that enable personalized prediction and adaptive control.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
791 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 317 0 R
/E (ABOUT THE SPEAKER)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
792 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 317 0 R
/E (Prof. Sitikantha Roy is a Fulbright-Nehru Academic & Professional Excellence Award winner and a joint faculty member in the Department of Applied Mechanics and the School of Artificial Intelligence at IIT Delhi, where he heads the Biomechanics and Soft Robotics Laboratory. His research focuses on human-centered dynamical systems, scientific-AI-driven hybrid predictive modeling, computational biomechanics, wearable robotics, and soft robotic systems. His work aims to bridge fundamental mechanics, computation, and experimentation to develop predictive technologies for healthcare, rehabilitation, and human augmentation.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
